@* WHY: two tabs sharing one spot, like Coursework. Why MIS is open first; #mccombs links open Why McCombs *@
<section class="pursue-part career-why" id="why-mis" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
    <span class="why-anchor" id="mccombs"></span>
    <div class="career-wrap">
        <h2 class="career-section-title" id="why-heading">The Why</h2>
        <div class="why-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Why">
            <button type="button" class="why-tab" role="tab" id="why-tab-mis" aria-controls="why-panel-mis" aria-selected="true">Why MIS</button>
            <button type="button" class="why-tab" role="tab" id="why-tab-mccombs" aria-controls="why-panel-mccombs" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Why McCombs</button>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="why-stack">
    <div class="career-wrap why-wrap why-panel" id="why-panel-mis" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="why-tab-mis">
        <div>
            <p class="why-question">I didn’t choose technology instead of the arts.<br />I chose it so my art could do more.</p>
            <p>Reading, writing, music and art have been constants for as long as I can remember. Eventually, making something beautiful stopped being enough. The more interesting question was why people chose it, remembered it, returned to it, or couldn’t put it down.</p>
            <p>And technology was already hiding inside everything I loved. The tools I draw with. The apps that put stories in my hands. The playlist that finds a song before I know I want it. None of those things are purely technical. Someone had to understand people well enough to imagine them, and technology well enough to make them real.</p>
            <p>That’s what Management Information Systems is to me.</p>
            <p><strong>I didn’t leave the humanities behind. I gave them a backend.</strong></p>
        </div>
            <div class="why-quotes" role="region" aria-roledescription="carousel" aria-label="Quotes I live by">
                <figure class="why-quote-slide">
                    <blockquote>It’s technology married with liberal arts, married with the humanities, that yields us the results that make our heart sing.</blockquote>
                    <figcaption>Steve Jobs</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <figure class="why-quote-slide" hidden>
                    <blockquote>The Analytical Engine weaves algebraical patterns just as the Jacquard loom weaves flowers and leaves.</blockquote>
                    <figcaption>Ada Lovelace</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <figure class="why-quote-slide" hidden>
                    <blockquote>The arts and sciences are avatars of human creativity.</blockquote>
                    <figcaption>Mae Jemison</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <figure class="why-quote-slide" hidden>
                    <blockquote>You can’t use up creativity. The more you use, the more you have.</blockquote>
                    <figcaption>Maya Angelou</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <figure class="why-quote-slide" hidden>
                    <blockquote>People ignore design that ignores people.</blockquote>
                    <figcaption>Frank Chimero</figcaption>
                </figure>
                <div class="why-quote-nav">
                    <button type="button" class="why-arrow" data-go="-1" aria-label="Previous quote">‹</button>
                    <div class="why-dots"><button type="button" aria-label="Quote 1" aria-current="true"></button><button type="button" aria-label="Quote 2"></button><button type="button" aria-label="Quote 3"></button><button type="button" aria-label="Quote 4"></button><button type="button" aria-label="Quote 5"></button></div>
                    <button type="button" class="why-arrow" data-go="1" aria-label="Next quote">›</button>
                </div>
            </div>
    </div>
    <div class="career-wrap why-panel" id="why-panel-mccombs" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="why-tab-mccombs" hidden>
        <div class="mccombs-grid">
            <ul class="mccombs-stats" aria-label="McCombs rankings, U.S. News &amp; World Report">
                <li>
                    <button type="button" class="mccombs-flip" aria-pressed="false">
                        <span class="mccombs-face front"><b>#1</b><span>Graduate Information Systems program in the US</span></span>
                        <span class="mccombs-face back"><em>What it means for me</em><span>The professors who teach my undergrad MIS classes teach in that graduate program too. I learn from the same people.</span></span>
                    </button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <button type="button" class="mccombs-flip" aria-pressed="false">
                        <span class="mccombs-face front"><b>#4</b><span>Management Information Systems, my major</span></span>
                        <span class="mccombs-face back"><em>What it means for me</em><span>It’s where I learned to build: full-stack apps, databases and AI features, not just the vocabulary.</span></span>
                    </button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <button type="button" class="mccombs-flip" aria-pressed="false">
                        <span class="mccombs-face front"><b>#5</b><span>Marketing, my minor</span></span>
                        <span class="mccombs-face back"><em>What it means for me</em><span>It’s where my Starbucks and Owala strategy work came from: the why behind what people buy.</span></span>
                    </button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <button type="button" class="mccombs-flip" aria-pressed="false">
                        <span class="mccombs-face front"><b>#6</b><span>Undergraduate business school in the US</span></span>
                        <span class="mccombs-face back"><em>What it means for me</em><span>It gives my tech the business side: what it costs, what it’s worth, and whether to build it at all.</span></span>
                    </button>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <button type="button" class="mccombs-flip" aria-pressed="false">
                        <span class="mccombs-face front"><b>Top 10</b><span>Ten of our BBA programs rank here</span></span>
                        <span class="mccombs-face back"><em>What it means for me</em><span>My team projects pair me with top-ranked finance, accounting and marketing students, so they run like a real company.</span></span>
                    </button>
                </li>
            </ul>
            <div class="mccombs-text">
                <p class="why-question">I didn’t come to Austin to study business from a textbook.</p>
                <p>I came to build things, break things, ask why people wanted them in the first place, and do it somewhere ambitious enough to keep up.</p>
                <p>My MIS major connects directly to professors who teach the #1 graduate Information Systems program in the country, giving me direct access to how technology and business actually intersect.</p>
                <p>Combining MIS with <a class="mccombs-build" href="#marketing"><b>Marketing</b></a> gave me the full picture: MIS taught me to build the technology behind an idea; Marketing taught me to understand the user on the other side—what they notice, trust, and care about. Austin’s proximity to industry and my summer at Oracle ensured none of it stayed theoretical.</p>
                <p>Here, I’ve built full-stack applications, designed and deployed databases, worked with real datasets, studied consumer behavior, and learned that the interesting question isn’t whether something can be built.</p>
                <p>It’s whether it should be built, who it’s for, and what would make them care.</p>
                <p>McCombs gave me the business. Austin gave me the playground.</p>
                @* typed live: the serious answer types out, deletes itself, and the joke about the rankings types in and stays *@
                <p class="mccombs-type">
                    <span class="mccombs-type-line is-on">A great business school isn’t a number on a ranking list. It’s what happens when you’re surrounded by people who keep raising your definition of good.</span>
                    <span class="mccombs-type-line">That said… someone went through all the trouble of ranking us. It would honestly be rude not to look.</span>
                </p>
                <script>
                    (() => {
                        const box = document.currentScript.previousElementSibling;
                        const [serious, joke] = box.querySelectorAll('.mccombs-type-line');
                        if (matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches || !('IntersectionObserver' in window)) return;
                        const texts = [serious.textContent, joke.textContent];
                        const wait = ms => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));
                        const caret = document.createElement('i');
                        caret.className = 'mccombs-caret';
                        serious.textContent = '';
                        serious.append(caret);
                        async function type(el, text, from = 0) {
                            caret.classList.add('typing');
                            for (let k = from + 1; k <= text.length; k++) {
                                el.textContent = text.slice(0, k);
                                el.append(caret);
                                const ch = text[k - 1];
                                // a beat on every pause, and the stretched letters drag on purpose
                                await wait(ch === '…' ? 520 : ch === '.' || ch === ',' ? 220 : ch === text[k - 2] ? 85 : 30);
                            }
                            caret.classList.remove('typing');
                        }
                        const seen = new IntersectionObserver(async ([e]) => {
                            if (!e.isIntersecting) return;
                            seen.disconnect();
                            await type(serious, texts[0]);
                            await wait(2600);
                            caret.classList.add('typing');
                            for (let t = texts[0]; t.length; ) { t = t.slice(0, -2); serious.textContent = t; serious.append(caret); await wait(12); }
                            await wait(500);
                            // the serious line keeps holding the space while hidden, so nothing below jumps
                            serious.textContent = texts[0];
                            serious.classList.remove('is-on');
                            joke.textContent = '';
                            joke.classList.add('is-on');
                            await type(joke, texts[1]);
                            // give them a moment to actually look, then check
                            await wait(5000);
                            await type(joke, texts[1] + ' hey, did you look yet??', texts[1].length);
                        }, { threshold: 0.6 });
                        seen.observe(box);
                    })();
                </script>
                <p><b>Texas doesn’t just think big—we build big, too.</b></p>
                <p class="mccombs-source">Tap a ranking to see what it means for me. Rankings: <a href="https://www.usnews.com/best-colleges/university-of-texas-at-austin-3658/overall-rankings" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. News &amp; World Report ↗</a></p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    </div>
</section>

